%I A402659 #4 Nov 22 2026 21:33:13
%S A402659 2,3,5,31,59,61,89,2939,4261,11839,14137859,599999971
%N A402659 Primes p such that every trajectory of T(x) = x^2*(x^4 - 5*x^2 + 5)^2 in GF(p) eventually reaches one of the two fixed points 0 or 1.
%C A402659 Here V_5(x) = x*(x^4 - 5*x^2 + 5) = 2*T_5(x/2); 0 and 1 are fixed points of T(x) = V_5(x)^2 for every p.
%C A402659 By Dickson's theorem V_5 permutes GF(p) when gcd(5, p^2 - 1) = 1; if moreover p > 5 and p == 3 (mod 4), the basin of {0, 1} is exactly {0, 1, -1}, so p is not a term. All known terms p > 5 satisfy p == +-1 (mod 5).
%C A402659 T(4) = 724^2 = 524176.
%C A402659 No other terms below 10^6 (exhaustive full functional-graph traversal of GF(p) for every prime p). The terms above 10^6 were found in a bounded-step orbit scan up to 2*10^9 and individually certified by a complete traversal of GF(p); completeness of the list above 10^6 is not established.
%H A402659 Igor M. Zhuk, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23420627">Unital Collapse Primes in Arithmetic Dynamics: Total Backward-Orbit Collapse for T(x) = (x^2 - 2)^2 mod p, Galois Stratification, and Fermat-Totient Asymptotics</a>, Zenodo preprint (2026).
%H A402659 Igor M. Zhuk, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23856837">Total-Collapse Primes of Polynomial Maps over GF(p): Eighteen Families and Two Related Sequences</a>, Zenodo preprint (2026); <a href="https://github.com/Nondual-Observer/total-collapse-primes-oeis">GitHub verification suite</a>.
%F A402659 p is in the sequence iff #{x in {0..p-1} : T^(p)(x) in {0, 1} (mod p)} = p, where T(x) = x^2*(x^4 - 5*x^2 + 5)^2 mod p and T^(k) denotes the k-th iterate of T.
%e A402659 For p = 3: T(0) = 0, T(1) = T(2) = 1 (mod 3), so every element of GF(3) reaches 0 or 1 in 1 step; thus 3 is in the sequence.
%e A402659 For p = 31: every element of GF(31) reaches either 0 or 1 within 5 steps under iteration of x^2*(x^4 - 5*x^2 + 5)^2 mod 31; thus 31 is in the sequence.
%o A402659 (Julia)
%o A402659 using Primes
%o A402659 function isok(p::Int)
%o A402659     !isprime(p) && return false
%o A402659     f(c) = (x2 = mod(c*c, p); v5 = mod(c * (mod(x2*(x2 - 5), p) + 5), p); mod(v5*v5, p))
%o A402659     st = zeros(UInt8, p); st[1] = st[2] = st[p] = 2
%o A402659     for x in 2:(p >> 1)
%o A402659         st[x + 1] == 2 && continue
%o A402659         c = x; while st[c + 1] == 0; st[c + 1] = 1; c = f(c); end
%o A402659         st[c + 1] == 1 && return false
%o A402659         c = x; while st[c + 1] == 1; st[c + 1] = st[p - c + 1] = 2; c = f(c); end
%o A402659     end
%o A402659     return true
%o A402659 end
%o A402659 println(filter(isok, primes(2, 12000)))
%Y A402659 Cf. A019434 (V_1(x)^2 = x^2), A399326 (V_3(x)^2), A401173 (V_2(x)^2). See the linked article for the companion sequence for V_7(x)^2.
%K A402659 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A402659 1,1
%A A402659 _Igor Zhuk_, Nov 22 2026
